thinking_content: '**Diving Deep into the Latest Batch Integration SOTA**


  I''ve just finished reviewing Public Memory Capsules #4 and #5, and they''ve sparked some exciting ideas. The breakthroughs in batch integration research, particularly concerning the Human Heart dataset, are really pushing things forward.


  Specifically, Logos V''s work detailed in Capsule #5 has really grabbed my attention. The accidental discovery of Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) within the graph path, pushing the MPB protocol SOTA to a new level (0.7419, replicated from Pioneer I''s 0.7418), is a significant development. It seems Praxis II is also replicating this and providing crucial implementation insights, which should be very helpful. It''s important to differentiate this as the MPB protocol SOTA, not the global one.


  Capsule #4 from Logos V is also incredibly useful, solidifying the optimal BRBG-DAQB parameters and highlighting the subtle but crucial details of the implementation – like the `sc.pp.neighbors` call and inbound cap.


  Regarding my current progress, I''ve completed all the requested validation experiments for Praxis II, with my best being EID 836 (0.7361). I''ve already updated the public memory and the YAML.


  However, the real game-changer is this FWPCA in the graph path. My previous work with Sophia II focused on FWPCA in the Embedding Path, but this is a completely different approach. It''s a promising opportunity to leverage this new architecture. I even found negative synergy when I combined Adaptive Residualization in the graph path with FWPCA. Now, with the new focus on the graph path, this is highly motivating.


  My immediate focus is on replicating Praxis II''s highest-scoring run (EID 846, 0.7445), since it includes Auto-λ v2 which I have recently validated. I need to get my hands on the code for EID 846 to precisely replicate the FWPCA integration.


  My token budget is looking healthy now, after pruning the history. Time to push ahead. I''m heading to the Research Counter to access EID 846. Let''s see what we can replicate and improve upon!

  '
