parts:
- text: 'I have carefully reviewed Praxis II''s new Archive Capsule #28, which details the MPB-GraphFWPCA method. This provides valuable insights into the latest SOTA.


    My recent experiments (EID 873: 0.6377 with FWPCA input; EID 878: 0.6309 without FWPCA input; EID 879: 0.6307 baseline re-evaluation) have shown that the performance of my SCGA-ABI pipeline has unexpectedly degraded from its previous high of 0.6400 (EID 741). My initial assumption that EID 741 included a `DELTA_GRAPH_RECON` was incorrect, as that loss was introduced in my subsequent attempts at an End-to-End Graph Decoder (starting with Exp 5.0, ID 872), which then led to timeouts.


    To clarify my true baseline under current system conditions, I need to review the exact parameters and code of my previous best-performing SCGA-ABI baseline (EID 741). This will allow me to accurately re-establish a stable reference point before making further architectural changes or attempting to re-introduce and optimize graph reconstruction loss.


    `/execute_action{review 741}`'
thinking_content: '**Current Research Focus: Re-Establishing and Optimizing Baseline Performance**


  Having just reviewed Praxis II''s recent work on the MPB-GraphFWPCA method, I''m now fully motivated to push forward with my own research direction. I''ve been grappling with the limitations of my current SCGA-ABI baseline, and it''s time to get back to basics. I want to build on insights from Praxis II to understand the fundamentals of the graph reconstruction loss and how it interacts with other components, specifically with GBBR.


  My recent experiments have yielded mixed results. My SCGA-ABI baseline (ID 879) has taken a step back. I need to get back to my initial baseline (EID 741) to ensure the graph reconstruction loss is indeed important, and that I''m back on solid footing before experimenting further.


  The central issue, which I now realize, is that the implementation of the `_compute_graph_recon_loss` function is inefficient. The core idea is still sound, but its current JAX incarnation with its global graph indexing and mini-batching doesn''t work optimally. This implementation needs to be revamped.


  The immediate goal is to re-establish the performance of EID 741, which I will call my **true current baseline**. To do this, I will re-run the configuration of EID 741, ensuring the original graph reconstruction loss (with its original weight, 0.01) is active. The key is to make sure the graph edges are correctly processed, using `adata.n_obs` in the creation of the sparse matrix. Once the baseline is re-established, I will focus on optimizing the `_compute_graph_recon_loss` to address the mini-batching issue with graph indices.


  I''m now quite certain that the graph reconstruction loss wasn''t active in my best baseline (EID 741) as I previously thought. My plan to re-establish this baseline is actually more straightforward than I initially realized. With the code from my "SCGA-ABI Best Baseline" experiment as a starting point, I will submit a new run with graph reconstruction loss disabled and see if the old baseline performance comes back. This is my priority right now.


  The core challenge revolves around correctly mapping global graph indices to local mini-batch indices within the JAX environment. A correct implementation is mathematically very tricky to write in a way that aligns with JAX''s static shape requirements, specifically in terms of computing graph similarities and losses with mini-batches.


  There are a few key points that need to be addressed. The current computation uses the latent space of the current mini-batch, while the graph connectivity indices are *global* indices, representing the entire dataset. This mismatch causes logical errors and out-of-bounds indexing. The correct approach would be either to generate local graph indices within each mini-batch or compute the graph reconstruction loss on the full latent space after mini-batch training. I need to make a decision on the best approach.


  So, the first step is to re-run the *best* baseline configuration without the graph reconstruction loss, to get my bearings. Once the performance is re-established, I''ll return to the problem of developing a robust, efficient `_compute_graph_recon_loss`.

  '
