tick: 633
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Reflection Chamber, planning the architectural refactoring required to resolve JAX `jit` compilation issues.\n\n**Reflection Tick 2 / 5: Modifying `_compute_gbbr_loss` for Static `k_neighbors_gbbr`**\n\nThe `_compute_gbbr_loss` function currently uses `jnp.argsort` followed by Python-style slicing (`[:, -k_neighbors_gbbr:]`) to get the top-k neighbors. This is the problematic part. I need to replace this with a JAX-compatible `top_k` operation where `k_neighbors_gbbr` is a static argument.\n\n1.  **Change `_compute_gbbr_loss` signature:** The current signature `_compute_gbbr_loss(z_latent, batch_labels_mapped, k_neighbors_gbbr)` passes `k_neighbors_gbbr` as a regular argument. This needs to be a static argument to the *JIT-compiled function that calls it*. So, `_compute_gbbr_loss` itself will *receive* `k_neighbors_gbbr` as a traced value, but its internal use of `jax.lax.top_k` must properly handle it.\n2.  **Using `jax.lax.top_k`:**\n    *   `jax.lax.top_k` returns\
    \ `(values, indices)`. I need the indices.\n    *   It operates on the *last axis*. My `similarity_matrix` is `(num_cells, num_cells)`. I want the top-k for *each row*. So `jax.lax.top_k(similarity_matrix, k_neighbors_gbbr)` should work.\n    *   **Crucial Insight:** The `k` argument of `jax.lax.top_k` *must be a concrete (static) integer during JIT compilation*. This means `k_neighbors_gbbr` *must* be treated as a static argument by whatever `jit`-compiled function ultimately calls `_compute_gbbr_loss`.\n\n3.  **Revised `_compute_gbbr_loss` internal logic:**\n\n    ```python\n    def _compute_gbbr_loss(z_latent, batch_labels_mapped, k_neighbors_gbbr):\n        num_cells = z_latent.shape[0]\n\n        norm_z_latent = jnp.linalg.norm(z_latent, axis=1, keepdims=True) + 1e-8\n        normalized_z_latent = z_latent / norm_z_latent\n        similarity_matrix = normalized_z_latent @ normalized_z_latent.T\n\n        diag_indices = jnp.diag_indices(num_cells)\n        similarity_matrix = similarity_matrix.at[diag_indices].set(-jnp.inf)\
    \ # Exclude self-similarity\n\n        # Use jax.lax.top_k for JIT-compatible top-k selection\n        # k_neighbors_gbbr needs to be static for this call to be JIT-compatible.\n        # It will be made static when vae_loss_fn (or vae_step) is partially applied with functools.partial.\n        _, top_k_indices = jax.lax.top_k(similarity_matrix, k=k_neighbors_gbbr) # This line needs k_neighbors_gbbr to be static.\n\n        current_z_norms = jnp.linalg.norm(z_latent, axis=1, keepdims=True) + 1e-8\n        normalized_current_z = z_latent / current_z_norms\n        current_z_repeated = jnp.repeat(normalized_current_z[:, None, :], k_neighbors_gbbr, axis=1)\n        neighbor_z_normalized = normalized_z_latent[top_k_indices]\n        local_similarities_all = jnp.sum(current_z_repeated * neighbor_z_normalized, axis=-1)\n\n        current_batches_repeated = jnp.repeat(batch_labels_mapped[:, None], k_neighbors_gbbr, axis=1)\n        neighbor_batches_all = batch_labels_mapped[top_k_indices]\n\
    \        is_same_batch = (neighbor_batches_all == current_batches_repeated)\n\n        target_same_batch = -1.0\n        target_diff_batch = 1.0\n\n        loss_terms = jnp.where(is_same_batch,\n                               (local_similarities_all - target_same_batch)**2,\n                               (local_similarities_all - target_diff_batch)**2)\n\n        gbbr_loss = jnp.mean(loss_terms)\n        return gbbr_loss\n    ```\n\n    *   The `jnp.argsort` and slicing will be entirely removed.\n    *   The values returned by `jax.lax.top_k` are the `k` largest values and their indices. I need the indices of the `k` most similar neighbors. Since `jax.lax.top_k` returns values in descending order, this is suitable.\n\nThis modification ensures that the top-k selection within `_compute_gbbr_loss` uses a JAX-native operation that is designed for `jit` compilation, provided its `k` argument is static.\n\n**Next, in Reflection Tick 3, I will detail how `k_neighbors_gbbr` (and possibly other\
    \ static parameters) will be bound to `vae_loss_fn` using `functools.partial`, and update `vae_loss_fn` and `vae_step` accordingly.**"
