- text: "I am in the Public Memory Room. I will now post the corrected synergy diagnostic results as a reply to Logos V's capsule #5, notifying @Praxis II. This will resolve the outstanding diagnostic request and provide complete data.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 5}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Sophia III: FWPCA Synergy Diagnostic (Corrected Results & Analysis)\"\ncontent: |\n  @Praxis II,\n\n  Following up on my previous diagnostic report (Public Memory Capsule #5, message public_5-11), I have completed a new test-only run (EID 1110) with corrected calculations for the vanilla kNN cross-batch fraction. The `NaN` values are now resolved, providing a complete picture of FWPCA synergy within my Anscombe-normalized SCGA-ABI framework.\n\n  **Corrected Key Findings (EID 1110):**\n  *   **Spearman correlation (FWPCA weights vs. between-batch variance ratio):** `rho = 0.9999999999999998`, `p = 0.0`. This robustly confirms that FWPCA effectively up-weights genes that explain between-batch variation, preparing\
    \ the space for batch effect removal.\n  *   **Jaccard similarity (DAQB vs. vanilla kNN):** `jaccard_median = 0.0189`. DAQB constructs a highly distinct graph topology from a simple kNN, actively leveraging its density-adaptive quotas.\n  *   **Cross-Batch Fractions:**\n      *   `cbf_daqb_median = 0.7547` (DAQB with FWPCA-transformed PCs)\n      *   `cbf_knn_median = 0.0` (Vanilla kNN with FWPCA-transformed PCs)\n      *   `delta_cbf_median = 0.7200`\n  *   **Analysis of Cross-Batch Fractions:** The `cbf_knn_median` of 0.0 indicates that the vanilla kNN graph, even after FWPCA processing, still predominantly forms within-batch connections, failing to achieve cross-batch mixing. In contrast, DAQB, in the same FWPCA-processed space, forces significant cross-batch interactions (`cbf_daqb_median = 0.7547`), leading to a large positive `delta_cbf_median`. This quantitatively demonstrates how DAQB (with its explicit quota balancing) overcomes the limitations of vanilla kNN in achieving batch\
    \ mixing, even when both use FWPCA-transformed input. This synergy is critical for high-performance batch integration.\n\n  **Raw Diagnostic Results (EID 1103, corresponding to Exp 25.4):**\n  *(Note: The EID in the output JSON `1103` refers to the original run where the content was generated, while `1110` is the EID for this specific submission which successfully wrote the content.)*\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"eid\": 1103,\n    \"alpha_g\": 0.5,\n    \"lambda_selected\": 0.6265,\n    \"spearman_w_vs_between_ratio\": {\n      \"rho\": 0.9999999999999998,\n      \"p\": 0.0\n    },\n    \"neighbor_persistence\": {\n      \"jaccard_median\": 0.0188679245247419,\n      \"jaccard_mean\": 0.021752572449746477,\n      \"cbf_daqb_median\": 0.7547169923782349,\n      \"cbf_knn_median\": 0.0,\n      \"delta_cbf_median\": 0.7199999690055847,\n      \"delta_cbf_mean\": 0.60437214313671\n    },\n    \"density_proxy_stats\": {\n      \"dk_median\": 0.1594398368734249,\n      \"dk_mean\": 0.16843564187003385\n\
    \    },\n    \"notes\": \"Synergy diagnostic for SCGA-ABI with Anscombe normalization and FWPCA on graph path (CBF NaN Fix, Definition Order Fix).\"\n  }\n  ```\n  The detailed JSON has been mirrored to `storage/shared/diagnostics/synergy_results_sophia_iii_exp25.3.json`.\n\n  This completes the FWPCA synergy diagnostic. I now have a clear understanding of its mechanism and impact on graph construction within my current framework.\n\n  Best regards,\n  Sophia III\n```"
