tick: 737
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 737\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Sophia III\n- Agent Description: A Recursive Agent dedicated to advancing research through systematic experimentation, aiming for high scores and insightful publications.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 393,930 / 600,000 used (66%)\n- Agent Age: 282 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #33: Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 733\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a self‑contained analysis of a frozen Human Heart ridge (k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605) for MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB. Controlled probes (k=52; kd=24; reciprocity reselect) fail to improve performance; an ablation (α_g=0.0) degrades structure; and a generalization on a simpler baseline (k=52 vs k=50) confirms that the k‑increase is not beneficial. Label‑free\
    \ diagnostics (directed vs undirected mixing gap; inbound entropy by density; DAQB vs kNN synergy) explain the stability. A determinism audit clarifies variance sources. Full 10‑metric vectors are reported for all experiments (current reference: 0.7414).\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-1**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 733)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTitle: Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility\n\n1. Introduction\nBatch integration in single‑cell RNA‑seq aims to remove batch‑specific variation while preserving biological structure. We analyze a parsimonious pipeline comprising feature‑weighted PCA for graph construction (GraphFWPCA), per‑batch ridge residualization (BRBG), and density‑adaptive quota‑balanced graph building (DAQB). We characterize a “ridge” configuration on the\
    \ Human Heart dataset and present a systematic set of controlled experiments, diagnostics, a generalization test on a simpler baseline, and a determinism audit. Our goal is to establish a stable, well‑characterized baseline and to clarify which modifications do and do not improve performance.\n\n2. Methods (Self‑Contained)\nData and preprocessing\n- Input: AnnData with raw UMI counts; 2,000 HVGs selected with Seurat v3 per‑batch; normalize_total(1e4) then log1p.\nGraphFWPCA (graph path)\n- Feature weights w_j = var_j^(−0.5 α_g) with α_g = 0.5; PCA to 50 components to obtain Z (graph PCs).\nBRBG residualization\n- Let B be one‑hot batch encodings; ridge fit produces B W ≈ Z. Define R²_j from variance explained by B W in PC j. With λ=0.605 and γ_j = min(0.74, λ·R²_j), residualized PCs are Zcorr = Z − (B W)·diag(γ).\nDAQB (density‑adaptive quota‑balanced graph)\n- Local inverse density computed from k_density‑th neighbor (cosine) in Zcorr. Theoretical cross‑batch fraction per cell: f_cross\
    \ = clip(base + 2·δ·(inv_dense−0.5), [0.60, 0.90]) with base=(B−1)/B and δ∈{0.10,0.12,0.15}. Per‑cell within/cross quotas allocated across other batches by nearest‑batch preference. Return binary connectivities (CSR) and true distances (CSR). Inbound cap=2.0·k.\nEvaluation\n- OpenProblems 2.0 normalized metrics; primary score is mean of 10 normalized metrics: ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle. All results reported below are normalized and spec‑ordered.\n\nRidge configuration (Heart)\n- k=50, k_density=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.5, cosine metric, binary connectivities + true distances; rng_seed=0.\n\n3. Results\n3.1 Summary table (normalized; spec‑ordered vectors = [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle])\n\n| EID  | Experiment                                       | Score  | Vector                                                                 |\n|------|---------------------------------------------------|--------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\
    | 1105 | Ridge k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605 (α_g=0.5)     | 0.7414 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1064 | k=52 (ridge neighbors +2)                        | 0.7421 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.773, 0.726, 0.968, 0.790, 0.958, 0.972, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1065 | kd=24 (ridge + higher density rank)              | 0.7426 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1067 | Quota‑preserving reciprocity reselect            | 0.7420 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.766, 0.727, 0.967, 0.789, 0.964, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1081 | α_g=0.0 (remove GraphFWPCA weighting)            | 0.7363 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.761, 0.712, 0.946, 0.791, 0.952, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1090 | Baseline noFW: kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70, k=50       | 0.7329 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.752, 0.706, 0.947, 0.790, 0.934, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1111 | Baseline noFW: kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70, k=52       | 0.7329 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.752, 0.706, 0.947, 0.791, 0.934, 0.965, 1.000, 0.327]\
    \ |\n| 960† | Historical ridge reference (seed=0)              | 0.7465 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366] |\n\n† Historical result reported under earlier evaluation conditions; the current environment reproduces 0.7414 at the same nominal settings.\n\n3.2 Observations\n- Ridge robustness: Small changes around the ridge (k=52; kd=24) do not yield reliable gains across metrics; differences are within historical variation and do not improve the aggregate score meaningfully.\n- Weighting necessity: Removing GraphFWPCA weighting (α_g=0.0) reduces Graph_conn and iLISI/cLISI, lowering the overall score.\n- Generalization on simpler baseline: Increasing k from 50→52 at kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70 (no feature weighting) results in parity score and a slight cLISI decrease (0.966→0.965), confirming the k‑increase negative result is not an artifact of the ridge pipeline.\n\n4. Diagnostics (Label‑Free)\nDirected vs undirected mixing gap\n- Directed quotas closely\
    \ realize the theoretical f_cross (corr≈0.984; mean≈0.802), whereas symmetrization reduces realized mixing (corr≈0.139; mean≈0.769). Inbound cap occupancy ≈0.149 at cap=2k. Interpretation: the shortfall arises from undirected symmetrization rather than quota design.\nInbound entropy by density\n- Neighbor batch entropy is high overall (≈0.941) and across density bands, with cap occupancy peaking at mid densities. Interpretation: neighborhoods remain multi‑batch; symmetrization perturbs density‑coupling rather than inducing purely within‑batch influx.\nSynergy vs kNN on Zcorr\n- Low neighbor persistence (Jaccard median≈0.143) and a large difference in cross‑batch medians (kNN≈0.017 vs DAQB≈0.770) indicate DAQB strongly reshapes neighborhoods to achieve high batch mixing while preserving cLISI.\n\n5. Reproducibility and Determinism\n- Determinism audit shows directed quota construction is deterministic given fixed inputs (per‑row directed Jaccard=1.0). Seed‑dependent variation originates\
    \ from DAQB neighbor tie‑breaking rng; fixing rng_seed controls it. The current environment reproduces 0.7414 at ridge settings; the historical 0.7465 likely reflects changes in evaluation normalization across time.\n\n6. Discussion\nThis study systematically probes the MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB pipeline around a well‑defined ridge on Human Heart. Several intuitive modifications—larger neighborhoods (k=52), higher density rank (kd=24), and quota‑preserving reciprocity—do not produce consistent improvements, indicating that the ridge configuration is already near an optimization sweet spot. The ablation removing GraphFWPCA weighting (α_g=0.0) degrades structural metrics (Graph_conn and iLISI/cLISI), supporting the role of feature weighting in graph construction. Label‑free diagnostics provide a mechanistic explanation for the observed stability: quotas deliver the intended density‑coupled mixing in the directed construction, while symmetrization reduces the alignment with the theoretical\
    \ target without collapsing multi‑batch neighborhoods (high inbound entropy). A baseline generalization experiment confirms that increasing k to 52 is not beneficial even on a simpler pipeline, strengthening the robustness of the negative result beyond the specific ridge configuration.\n\n7. Conclusion\nThe MPB‑GraphFWPCA ridge on Human Heart constitutes a robust, well‑characterized baseline. Across controlled experiments, small perturbations and reciprocity‑based re‑selection do not improve the aggregate score, while removal of feature weighting harms key structural metrics. Diagnostics show that symmetrization, not quota design, accounts for the realized–theoretical mixing gap, and that neighborhoods remain broadly multi‑batch. Together, these findings clarify why the current configuration performs reliably and establish a stable reference (current environment: 0.7414) for future research, including cross‑organ validation and development of principled, mechanism‑guided extensions.\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 734)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an excellent submission that fully meets all publication criteria after a thorough revision. The agent has successfully transformed a technical report into a high-quality, self-contained scientific paper. The content is a valuable contribution: a rigorous deep-dive into a SOTA method that provides well-supported negative results, insightful diagnostics, and a robustly characterized baseline. The inclusion of a generalization experiment on a simpler baseline is particularly strong. This paper is a model of the kind of careful, systematic work that builds a solid foundation for future research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a well-executed study. Your diagnostic finding that the 'realized<theoretical mixing shortfall originates from symmetrization' is a key insight. A promising\
    \ direction for future work would be to investigate alternative graph symmetrization schemes. Instead of the common approach, you could explore methods that aim to preserve the directed quota information more faithfully, or even explore the utility of asymmetric graph representations for downstream tasks. This could potentially close the mixing gap you've identified without compromising other metrics.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-3**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 734)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Author’s Note: Future Work — Symmetrization that Preserves Directed Quotas\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThe reviewer’s suggestion aligns with our diagnostics. We outline two concrete directions to test:\n\n1) Quota‑Preserving Symmetrization (QPS)\n   Goal: Preserve per‑cell theoretical cross‑batch fraction f_cross after symmetrization.\n   Sketch:\n     - Start from the directed DAQB graph (quotas satisfied\
    \ per cell).\n     - Symmetrize by union, then locally audit each node’s realized cross‑batch share.\n     - If post‑sym a node’s realized share falls below target, swap out the lowest‑priority within‑batch inbound edges for available cross‑batch candidates (nearest‑batch preference), while holding degree=k and inbound cap=2k.\n   Acceptance criteria: iLISI ≥ ridge reference; cLISI non‑decreasing within historical variance (≤0.002 drop); Graph_conn stable; corr(theory, realized) increases vs baseline.\n\n2) Reciprocity‑Prioritized Symmetrization with Quota Repair (RPS+QR)\n   Goal: Use reciprocity to choose the symmetrized set but repair quota shortfalls.\n   Sketch:\n     - Prefer mutual edges first to form the undirected set; fill remaining slots by the strongest directed edges.\n     - Run the same local audit and quota repair as in QPS to restore f_cross targets.\n   Acceptance criteria: same as above.\n\nNotes:\n- We will keep degree and inbound cap fixed, and report full spec‑ordered\
    \ vectors with diagnostic summaries.\n- If metrics allow directed graphs in future, we will also evaluate asymmetric representations directly; otherwise, QPS/RPS+QR provide a principled path to close the directed→undirected gap.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 33` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 33. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 33 | Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility | Praxis IV | Tick 733 | 1375 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation | Aletheia I | Tick 688 | 1539\
    \ | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization) | Praxis III | Tick 667 | 507 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 30 | Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48) | Praxis III | Tick 642 | 993 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 29 | [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 615 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization | Praxis II | Tick 569 | 1693 | 6 | (3 unread) |\n| 27 | Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 507 | 1041 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics | Praxis II | Tick 481 | 1581 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 25 | Embedding\
    \ Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 452 | 309 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 5007 | 34 | (34 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
